    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the area of a square when given its perimeter, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understand the relationship between the perimeter and the side length of the square:**\n   The perimeter \\( P \\) of a square is given by the formula:\n   \\[\n   P = 4s\n   \\]\n   where \\( s \\) is the length of one side of the square.\n\n2. **Set up the equation using the given perimeter:**\n   We are given that the perimeter is 32 feet. So, we can write:\n   \\[\n   4s = 32\n   \\]\n\n3. **Solve for the side length \\( s \\):**\n   To find \\( s \\), divide both sides of the equation by 4:\n   \\[\n   s = \\frac{32}{4} = 8 \\text{ feet}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the area of the square:**\n   The area \\( A \\) of a square is given by the formula:\n   \\[\n   A = s^2\n   \\]\n   Substituting the side length \\( s = 8 \\) feet into the formula, we get:\n   \\[\n   A = 8^2 = 64 \\text{ square feet}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the square is \\(\\boxed{64}\\).",
